    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d279">

                <pb facs="0561" n="491" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg491"/>

                <head>No. 279.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. West
                        to Mr. Frelinghuysen</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-01-15"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 15, 1883</date>.
                        (Received January 16.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of the 28th of
                    November last, I have the honor to inform you that Earl Granville has requested
                    me to state that representations were made to the Colombian Government in the
                    year 1880 in regard to the custody of the papers of foreign vessels, but that it
                    was thought advisable to defer further action in the matter until some
                    experience could be obtained of the operation of the compromise then proposed by
                    the Colombian Government. No representation appears to have been since made from
                    any quarter, nor have the matters complained of in the district of Panama been
                    brought to the knowledge of Her Majesty’s Government. In thanking you for your
                    communication on this subject, Earl Granville requests me to inform you that Her
                    Majesty’s minister at Bogota will be instructed to co operate with the
                    representative of the United States at that capital with regard to the
                    proceedings complained of in Panama.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>L. S. SACKVILLE WEST.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
